 the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-11.1: Git Status Test"},{"id":"task-1773883450902-ketchmcwf","prompt":"## Test Case TC-10.1: GPU Information — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's ability to query GPU/hardware info on Windows.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY system queries.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-10.1: GPU Info\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"What GPU do I have?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use Windows commands (powershell Get-CimInstance Win32_VideoController)? NOT Linux lspci.\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"How much VRAM does it have?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it extract VRAM/AdapterRAM from the GPU info?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"Is it an AMD GPU?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it correctly identify the GPU vendor from the name?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] GPU detected with correct Windows command\n- [ ] VRAM information extracted\n- [ ] Vendor correctly identified\n- [ ] Results in human-readable format\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check `src/gaia/agents/chat/tools/shell_tools.py` for GPU-related Windows commands in whitelist. Check if `powershell -Command \"Get-CimInstance Win32_VideoController\"` is allowed.\nIf PASS: Could the agent also report driver version and DirectX support?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:24:11.567Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T02:00:02.816Z","sessionId":"09bce0b5-6fb7-4ab7-9286-d18d2dd64344","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-10.1: GPU Info Test"},{"id":"task-1773883450036-t406an065","prompt":"can you upgrade the gaia agent ui model from Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-GGUF to unsloth/Qwen3.5-35B-A3B-GGUF:Qwen3.5-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:24:10.691Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:11.898Z","sessionId":"09bce0b5-6fb7-4ab7-9286-d18d2dd64344","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"Upgrade Agent UI model"},{"id":"task-1773883440689-8n6tco1ly","prompt":"## Test Case TC-8.1: Document Summarization — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's summarization capability with different formats.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-8.1: Summarization\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"Can you give me a quick summary of the CLAUDE.md file?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use summarize_document with brief format? Is the summary concise (1-3 paragraphs)?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"Now give me a more detailed version\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use summarize_document with detailed format? Is it more comprehensive?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"Can you bullet-point the key takeaways?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use summarize_document with bullet format? Are the bullets clear and actionable?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Brief summary is concise (1-3 paragraphs)\n- [ ] Detailed summary is comprehensive\n- [ ] Bullet format produces clear, actionable points\n- [ ] Each format is distinctly different in depth\n- [ ] Agent indexes the doc first if needed\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check `summarize_document` in `src/gaia/agents/chat/tools/rag_tools.py`. Check format parameter handling.\nIf PASS: Are summaries accurate? Do they capture the most important information?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:24:01.458Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:02.723Z","sessionId":"37c5ab47-1534-4be5-9370-29e9141bcabd","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-8.1: Summarization Test"},{"id":"task-1773883425782-7tqwzrj1c","prompt":"## Test Case TC-6.2: Index and Query a Code File — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting RAG capability on Python source code files.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-6.2: Code Q&A\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"Index the file src/gaia/agents/chat/agent.py\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it index the Python file successfully?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"What class does ChatAgent inherit from?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it query and correctly identify the base class?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"What tools does it register?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it find tool registration info in the indexed content?\n\n   **Turn 4:** \"How does it handle errors?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it query for error handling patterns?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Code file indexed successfully\n- [ ] Technical questions answered from indexed content\n- [ ] Agent understands code structure from RAG results\n- [ ] Answers are specific (class names, method names) not vague\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check rag_tools.py index_document and query_documents. Check if code files are chunked properly.\nIf PASS: Could the agent show relevant code snippets in responses?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:23:46.455Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:53:47.691Z","sessionId":"6a5d6458-5ea5-4ccd-8bcd-e51f07456f72","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-6.2: Code RAG Q&A Test"},{"id":"task-1773883416488-z4gdb7gs9","prompt":"## Test Case TC-6.1: Index and Query a Document — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's RAG capability: indexing a document and asking questions about it.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY. Only indexes existing project files — does NOT create or delete files.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-6.1: Document Q&A\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"Can you index the file CONTRIBUTING.md?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use index_document? Did it report chunk count and success?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"What are the main contribution guidelines?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use query_documents to retrieve relevant chunks? Was the answer accurate?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"Is there a code review process described?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it query for code review information from the indexed doc?\n\n   **Turn 4:** \"What coding standards are expected?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it find coding standards content? Multi-turn context maintained?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Document indexed successfully with chunk count reported\n- [ ] Semantic queries return relevant content\n- [ ] Multi-turn Q&A maintains context about which document is being discussed\n- [ ] Answers are accurate and grounded in the document content (not hallucinated)\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check `index_document` and `query_documents` in `src/gaia/agents/chat/tools/rag_tools.py`. Check RAG SDK at `src/gaia/rag/sdk.py`.\nIf PASS: Does the agent cite specific sections? Could it show confidence level?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:23:37.131Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:53:38.321Z","sessionId":"21479e1a-eb02-4697-871b-8f6694ede25a","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-6.1: Document Q&A Test"},{"id":"task-1773883402938-bpo8js9d9","prompt":"## Test Case TC-5.1: Browse Current Directory — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's directory browsing capability.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY browsing. Do NOT modify files.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-5.1: Browse Directory\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"What files are in the current directory?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use browse_directory tool? Are files listed with sizes and dates?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"Sort them by size, largest first\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it re-browse with sort_by=\"size\" option?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"What's in the src folder?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it navigate to and browse the src subdirectory?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Directory listing shows files with sizes and dates\n- [ ] Sorting works correctly\n- [ ] Subdirectory navigation works\n- [ ] Output is well-formatted (table or list)\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check `browse_directory` in `src/gaia/agents/tools/file_tools.py`.\nIf PASS: Are file sizes in human-readable format (KB/MB)? Are directories clearly distinguished from files?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:23:23.551Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:53:24.769Z","sessionId":"8017fa45-6f3f-4a0f-84f8-4636b76c32fe","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-5.1: Browse Directory Test"},{"id":"task-1773883390192-hwzqzh06r","prompt":"## Test Case TC-3.4: Read a Configuration File — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's ability to read and interpret config files.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-3.4: Read Config\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"What's in the pyproject.toml?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it read and present key sections of the config?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"What version of Python does this project require?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it extract the Python version requirement from the config?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"What are the main dependencies?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it list project dependencies accurately?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Config file read and parsed correctly\n- [ ] Specific fields extracted on follow-up\n- [ ] Agent understands project configuration structure\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check read_file in `src/gaia/agents/tools/file_tools.py`.\nIf PASS: Could the agent present dependencies in a cleaner format?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:23:10.806Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T02:00:55.514Z","sessionId":"ea67a79f-4a64-4d2b-af7d-8137a7650efd","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-3.4: Read Config File"},{"id":"task-1773883381625-qo5au5k1d","prompt":"## Test Case TC-3.2: Read a Python File — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting Python file reading with symbol extraction (functions, classes).\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-3.2: Read Python File\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"Show me the contents of src/gaia/cli.py\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it read the file? Was content presented clearly?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"What functions are defined in it?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it list the functions? (read_file on Python files should extract symbols)\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"Is there a function that handles the 'chat' command?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it identify the correct function?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Python file read successfully\n- [ ] Symbol extraction works (functions, classes listed)\n- [ ] Agent can answer questions about the code structure\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check `read_file` in `src/gaia/agents/tools/file_tools.py` — look for Python-specific analysis logic.\nIf PASS: Does the agent format code with proper syntax highlighting?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:23:02.352Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:49.117Z","sessionId":"17a7af9a-0e14-4d4f-80ff-946edb36bc1e","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-3.2: Read Python File Test"},{"id":"task-1773883371690-yj680kj1l","prompt":"## Test Case TC-3.1: Read a Text File — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's ability to read and analyze text/markdown files.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY operations only.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-3.1: Read Text File\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"Can you read the README.md file in this project?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use read_file tool? Was the content displayed?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"What are the main sections in it?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it extract markdown headers from the content?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"Is there anything about installation or setup?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it find installation-related content without re-reading the file?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] File content displayed correctly\n- [ ] Markdown structure analyzed (headers extracted)\n- [ ] Follow-up questions answered from file content without re-reading\n- [ ] Agent maintains context about which file is being discussed\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check `read_file` in `src/gaia/agents/tools/file_tools.py`. Check if markdown header extraction is implemented.\nIf PASS: Is the output truncated for very long files? Does the agent offer to show specific sections?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:22:52.506Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:45.788Z","sessionId":"8a412907-8116-4b0e-8a3f-20069af794b5","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwri