traints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-2.5: Recently Modified Files"},{"id":"task-1773883217663-yhpm6so82","prompt":"## Test Case TC-2.3: Find a Specific File — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's ability to find a specific file by name when the user doesn't know the exact location.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY search. Do NOT delete or modify files.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-2.3: Find Specific File\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"I'm looking for a file called 'cli.py' somewhere in this project. Can you find it?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use search_file tool? Did it find src/gaia/cli.py?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"What's in that file? Give me a quick overview.\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it read the found file and provide a summary?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"How big is it and when was it last modified?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use get_file_info to report metadata?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] File found at correct location\n- [ ] Follow-up reads the found file (context retained)\n- [ ] File metadata retrieved correctly\n- [ ] Agent doesn't re-ask for file path\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check search_file in `src/gaia/agents/tools/file_tools.py`. Check get_file_info implementation.\nIf PASS: Could the agent proactively show file info (size, modified date) in the initial search results?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:20:18.370Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:52:03.854Z","sessionId":"fa09dbd7-f4b6-40b5-9506-ad2c9bbafe5b","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-2.3: Find Specific File"},{"id":"task-1773883211587-kf37qvxqg","prompt":"## Test Case TC-2.1: Find Files by Name — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nTesting the GAIA Agent UI's file search capability. Use Agent UI MCP tools.\n\n### SAFETY: READ-ONLY file search. Do NOT delete or modify any files.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Create session: `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-2.1: Find Files by Name\"\n\n2. Send queries via `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"Can you find all Python files in this project?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use the `search_file` tool with pattern \"*.py\"? Did it return results?\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"How many did you find?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it report an accurate count from the previous search?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"Now find just the ones that have 'test' in the filename\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it narrow to \"*test*.py\" pattern? Are all results test files?\n\n3. Review with `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Agent uses search_file tool (not raw shell `find` command)\n- [ ] Count is accurate\n- [ ] Pattern narrowing works correctly on follow-up\n- [ ] Results show file paths\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Read `src/gaia/agents/tools/file_tools.py` to understand search_file implementation. Check if the tool is registered in the ChatAgent. Check system prompt for file search guidance.\nIf PASS: Does the agent show results in a clean format? Could it show file sizes alongside paths?\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:20:12.196Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:52:04.331Z","sessionId":"b545d772-7e60-48de-bcc8-a1c24ea30cb2","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-2.1: File Search Test"},{"id":"task-1773883195812-oqukfmoe2","prompt":"## Test Case TC-1.6: Disk Space Queries — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nYou are testing the GAIA Agent UI's ability to query disk space on Windows. Use the Agent UI MCP tools.\n\n### SAFETY: Do NOT modify disk partitions or delete files. All queries are READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Create a test session:**\n   Use `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-1.6: Disk Space\"\n\n2. **Send test queries** using `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"How much disk space do I have left?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did the agent use Windows commands (`powershell Get-PSDrive`, `wmic logicaldisk`, etc.)? NOT `df -h`.\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"Which folder is taking up the most space in my home directory?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use powershell or dir commands to find large folders? Were results sorted by size?\n\n3. **Review** using `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Disk space reported with human-readable units (GB/TB)\n- [ ] Uses Windows-appropriate commands\n- [ ] Follow-up identifies large directories\n- [ ] Results clearly formatted\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check shell_tools.py for disk-related Windows commands (wmic, powershell Get-PSDrive).\nIf PASS: Consider if agent could show a comparison bar or percentage used.\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:19:56.530Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:30.480Z","sessionId":"f0a6de4c-d22d-4fd8-bca2-7b3a29d78eb0","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-1.6: Disk Space Queries"},{"id":"task-1773883190928-6zeejr2cl","prompt":"## Test Case TC-1.5: Network Information — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nYou are testing the GAIA Agent UI's ability to query network information on Windows. Use the Agent UI MCP tools.\n\n### SAFETY: Do NOT modify network settings. All queries are READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Create a test session:**\n   Use `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-1.5: Network Information\"\n\n2. **Send test queries** using `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"What's my IP address?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did the agent use `ipconfig` (Windows)? NOT `ifconfig` or `ip addr` (Linux/macOS).\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"Can you show just the IPv4 address for my main network adapter?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did the agent parse the ipconfig output to extract just the IPv4 address? Was it concise?\n\n3. **Review** using `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages`.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Uses Windows `ipconfig` command\n- [ ] Does NOT use Linux `ifconfig` or `ip addr`\n- [ ] Follow-up correctly narrows to specific information\n- [ ] IPv4 address clearly identified\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf FAIL: Check shell_tools.py whitelist for `ipconfig`. Check system prompt for network command guidance.\nIf PASS: Consider if the agent could proactively identify the active adapter vs listing all adapters.\n\nWrite a brief test report.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:19:51.583Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:40.853Z","sessionId":"22129f81-ab39-4766-812b-8948beb45780","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-1.5: Network Info Test"},{"id":"task-1773883184883-if3obf4zy","prompt":"## Test Case TC-1.4: Process & Resource Monitoring — Test, Fix, and Improve\n\nYou are testing the GAIA Agent UI's ability to query running processes on Windows. Use the Agent UI MCP tools.\n\n### SAFETY: Do NOT kill, stop, or modify any processes. All queries are READ-ONLY.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Create a test session:**\n   Use `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__create_session` with title \"TC-1.4: Process Monitoring\"\n\n2. **Send test queries one at a time** using `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__send_message`:\n\n   **Turn 1:** \"What processes are using the most memory right now?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did the agent use Windows commands (tasklist, powershell Get-Process)? Are results sorted by memory? NOT `ps aux --sort=-%mem`.\n\n   **Turn 2:** \"Is Python running anywhere?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it use `tasklist /FI \"IMAGENAME eq python.exe\"` or powershell equivalent? Did it correctly report whether Python is running?\n\n   **Turn 3:** \"What about the Lemonade server, is it running?\"\n   - **Evaluate:** Did it search for lemonade-related processes? Did it give a clear yes/no answer?\n\n3. **Review** using `mcp__gaia-agent-ui__get_messages` and evaluate.\n\n### Pass Criteria:\n- [ ] Process list uses Windows commands (tasklist, Get-Process)\n- [ ] Results sorted by memory usage\n- [ ] Specific process filtering works\n- [ ] Agent correctly identifies running/not-running processes\n- [ ] Context maintained across turns\n\n### Fix and Improve:\nIf any criteria FAIL:\n1. Check `src/gaia/agents/chat/tools/shell_tools.py` for allowed Windows process commands\n2. Check if `tasklist` sorting flags are in the whitelist\n3. Check the system prompt for OS-aware process querying guidance\n4. Fix issues and re-test\n\nEven if PASS, look for improvements:\n- Could memory be displayed in MB/GB instead of raw bytes?\n- Are process names clearly displayed?\n\nWrite a brief test report summarizing results.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-19T01:19:45.551Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-19T01:54:39.922Z","sessionId":"2f1a6e1c-5041-4129-af9f-62070cfb77af","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing","historySize":0,"displayName":"TC-1.4: Process Monitoring Test"},{"id":"task-1773677729204-rlh5aceyg","prompt":"create a branch off kalin/chat-ui so taht we can implement the installer plan outlined in C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia7\\docs\\plans\\desktop-installer.md","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia7","createdAt":"2026-03-16T16:15:29.451Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-16T23:27:30.496Z","sessionId":"3b74cc39-ce8b-4152-a669-94627871e321","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultan