ry 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774474561613-myb5flx2b\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1774474561613-myb5flx2b):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774474561613-myb5flx2b\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1774474561613-myb5flx2b\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Research Competitive Claims"},{"id":"task-1774474582738-2n1qhli7w","prompt":"You are a deep web researcher. Research and verify the following claims about AMD hardware, local AI inference economics, and the AI PC market. Provide specific data points, sources, and evidence for each claim. Use WebSearch and WebFetch extensively. Search multiple times with different queries to find the best data. Write your findings as structured markdown with citations.\n\nCLAIMS TO VERIFY:\n1. \"Running agents locally eliminates $600-$3,600+/year in cloud API costs\" - Research actual cloud API costs vs local inference costs for AI agents\n2. \"Ryzen AI 50+ TOPS NPU\" - Verify AMD Ryzen AI NPU specifications\n3. \"6 major OEMs shipping Ryzen AI 400\" - Verify which OEMs are shipping Ryzen AI 400 series\n4. \"Halo Box / Ryzen AI Halo Developer Platform\" - Verify this product exists and its specs\n5. \"MI300X\" data center GPU claims - Verify MI300X specs and positioning\n6. \"Ryzen AI Max+ delivers the highest tokens-per-second-per-dollar for local inference\" - Search for inference benchmarks\n7. \"ROCm\" as open-source GPU compute stack - Verify ROCm status and capabilities vs CUDA\n8. \"AMD Developer Cloud\" - Verify this service exists and its offerings\n9. \"Open-weight models close the quality gap with cloud models for agent tasks\" by 2027 - Research open-weight model progress (Llama, Qwen, Mistral, DeepSeek)\n10. \"Lemonade Server\" and \"PR #53865\" to OpenClaw - Search for AMD Lemonade Server\n\nAlso research: \"AMD Ryzen AI 400 specs\", \"AMD Halo Box\", \"Ryzen AI Max+ benchmarks\", \"MI300X vs H100 inference\", \"ROCm vs CUDA 2025 2026\", \"AMD Developer Cloud\", \"local LLM inference cost savings\", \"open weight models vs cloud models 2026\", \"AMD Lemonade Server\", \"AI PC market 2026\".\n\nSave your findings to: C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\Projects\\OpenClawStrategy\\research-amd-hardware-claims.md","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\Projects\\OpenClawStrategy","createdAt":"2026-03-25T21:36:23.282Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-25T21:50:54.070Z","sessionId":"e427b436-2ce4-4712-b8a4-e413bd3498d9","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774474582738-2n1qhli7w\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1774474582738-2n1qhli7w):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774474582738-2n1qhli7w\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1774474582738-2n1qhli7w\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Research AMD Hardware Claims"},{"id":"task-1774480724079-66y5p8hjp","prompt":"given this detailed strategy document, are there some non-obvious conclusions we can make?","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\Projects\\OpenClawStrategy","createdAt":"2026-03-25T23:18:44.885Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-26T09:21:45.483Z","sessionId":"b41109ab-5cbd-4135-ab48-86bd3caa30fb","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774480724079-66y5p8hjp\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1774480724079-66y5p8hjp):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774480724079-66y5p8hjp\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1774480724079-66y5p8hjp\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0},{"id":"task-1774474491586-ygmciwofi","prompt":"perform a deep web research on all claims and create an analysis report that provides evidence given the claims, make this anlaysis data driven. we will integrate into main strategy document but for now, lets create a seperate document while researching the claims and backing them with data and references.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\Projects\\OpenClawStrategy","createdAt":"2026-03-25T21:34:52.598Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-25T22:33:42.688Z","sessionId":"d44f9cb4-896a-4523-82d4-199720d76c30","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\