sk` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"LLM Context/Model Validation"},{"id":"task-1774285252089-pileyllxw","prompt":"Search the web for real-world LEGAL documents (regulations, licenses, terms of service, standards) and download them to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\legal\\`. These will be used in an agentic RAG eval benchmark.\n\n## Your Goal\nFind 3-4 publicly available legal documents with clear, verifiable content. Prefer varied formats and complexity.\n\n## Good Candidates (use WebSearch + WebFetch)\n1. **Apache License 2.0** — fetch from https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt (plain text, well-known legal document)\n2. **MIT License** text — fetch from https://opensource.org/license/mit (short, pure legal text)\n3. **GDPR Article 17 (Right to Erasure)** — search \"GDPR article 17 text site:gdpr-info.eu\" and fetch the specific article\n4. **A real Terms of Service excerpt** — search for a public company's ToS (e.g., GitHub ToS from https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/github-terms/github-terms-of-service) — fetch a specific section\n\n## Steps\n1. Use WebSearch to find good candidate URLs\n2. Use WebFetch to retrieve the actual content\n3. Save each document to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\legal\\`\n4. For long documents (like ToS), save only the most fact-dense sections (e.g., first 200 lines)\n5. Use filenames like `apache_license_2.0.txt`, `gdpr_article_17.txt`, `github_tos_excerpt.txt`\n\n## IMPORTANT for saving files\n- Use complete absolute Windows paths: `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\legal\\filename.txt`\n- Read each file back after writing to verify it saved correctly\n\n## Output Required\nReturn a JSON block listing each file and 2-3 ground-truth facts:\n```json\n{\n  \"documents\": [\n    {\n      \"filename\": \"apache_license_2.0.txt\",\n      \"format\": \"text\",\n      \"domain\": \"legal\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt\",\n      \"facts\": [\n        {\"question\": \"What version of the Apache License is this?\", \"answer\": \"Version 2.0\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What year was Apache License 2.0 published?\", \"answer\": \"January 2004\"},\n        {\"question\": \"Can you use Apache-licensed code commercially?\", \"answer\": \"Yes, commercial use is permitted\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-23T17:00:52.336Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-23T22:15:43.888Z","sessionId":"a9d93a4a-c2b3-4d62-ac3f-8949f6d2c4ab","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Download Legal Documents"},{"id":"task-1774285232520-k7o955nxn","prompt":"Search the web for real-world TECHNICAL documents (datasheets, specifications, RFCs, API docs) and download them to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\technical\\`. These will be used in an agentic RAG eval benchmark.\n\n## Your Goal\nFind 3-4 publicly available technical documents with clear, verifiable facts (numbers, specs, versions, dates). Prefer varied formats: PDF, TXT, HTML, or plain text.\n\n## Good Candidates (use WebSearch to find exact URLs, then WebFetch to download)\n1. **An RFC document** (plain text): e.g., RFC 2616 (HTTP/1.1) or RFC 7231 or RFC 9110. Fetch from https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfcXXXX.txt - these are pure text and perfect for RAG\n2. **A Raspberry Pi or Arduino hardware datasheet / spec page** - search for \"raspberry pi 4 specifications site:raspberrypi.com\" and fetch the HTML\n3. **USB specification overview page** or **Bluetooth specification summary** - find a public spec overview page\n4. **A real API changelog or technical release notes** - e.g., Python release notes for a specific version from docs.python.org\n\n## Steps\n1. Use WebSearch to find good candidate URLs for each document type\n2. Use WebFetch to retrieve the actual content\n3. Save each document to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\technical\\` using the Write tool with an appropriate filename (e.g., `rfc7231_http_semantics.txt`, `raspberry_pi4_specs.html`, etc.)\n4. Keep files under 50KB if possible - if a page is huge, extract/truncate the most fact-dense section and save that\n\n## IMPORTANT for saving files\n- Use complete absolute Windows paths: `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\technical\\filename.txt`\n- Read each file back after writing to verify it saved correctly\n\n## Output Required\nAfter saving all files, return a JSON block listing each file you saved and 2-3 ground-truth facts extracted from it:\n```json\n{\n  \"documents\": [\n    {\n      \"filename\": \"rfc7231_http_semantics.txt\",\n      \"format\": \"text\",\n      \"domain\": \"technical\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://...\",\n      \"facts\": [\n        {\"question\": \"What HTTP method is used to retrieve a resource?\", \"answer\": \"GET\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What status code means 'Not Found'?\", \"answer\": \"404\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-23T17:00:32.762Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-23T17:08:41.398Z","sessionId":"f2af4af9-3efd-4f1a-bcbd-c170f9100d01","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Download Technical Documents"},{"id":"task-1774285243778-vmtuf9kh1","prompt":"Search the web for real-world FINANCIAL documents (SEC filings, annual reports, earnings summaries) and download them to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\financial\\`. These will be used in an agentic RAG eval benchmark.\n\n## Your Goal\nFind 2-3 publicly available financial documents with clear, verifiable numbers (revenue, profit, dates, percentages). Prefer varied formats.\n\n## Good Candidates (use WebSearch + WebFetch)\n1. **SEC EDGAR 10-K or 10-Q filing** — search for a recent smaller-company filing. Try: `site:sec.gov 10-K annual report 2024 htm`. Edgar filings at https://www.sec.gov/cgi-bin/browse-edgar have HTML versions. Try fetching https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%2210-K%22&dateRange=custom&startdt=2024-01-01&enddt=2024-12-31&forms=10-K to find a filing, then fetch the HTML document. Look for a company with simple, readable financials (not hundreds of pages).\n2. **A US Treasury or Federal Reserve press release** — search \"site:federalreserve.gov press release 2024 interest rate\" and fetch a rates decision press release (these are short, factual, and perfect for RAG)\n3. **A World Bank or IMF country economic brief** — search \"site:worldbank.org country brief GDP 2024 PDF\" \n\n## Preferred approach for SEC\n- Try fetching https://efts.sec.gov/LATEST/search-index?q=%2210-K%22&forms=10-K&dateRange=custom&startdt=2024-06-01&enddt=2024-12-31 to find filings\n- Or try a direct URL like: https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/full-index/2024/QTR4/company.idx\n- Or search \"SEC EDGAR 10-K small company 2024 HTML filing\" and find a short one\n\n## Steps\n1. Use WebSearch to find candidate URLs\n2. Use WebFetch to retrieve content\n3. If content is very long, extract the key financial summary sections only (MD&A, financial highlights, revenue tables)\n4. Save each document to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\financial\\` using Write tool\n5. Keep files under 60KB\n\n## IMPORTANT for saving files\n- Use complete absolute Windows paths: `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\financial\\filename.txt`\n- Read each file back after writing to verify it saved correctly\n\n## Output Required\nReturn a JSON block listing each file and 2-3 ground-truth facts:\n```json\n{\n  \"documents\": [\n    {\n      \"filename\": \"fed_rate_decision_2024.txt\",\n      \"format\": \"text\",\n      \"domain\": \"financial\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://...\",\n      \"facts\": [\n        {\"question\": \"What rate did the Fed set in November 2024?\", \"answer\": \"4.5-4.75%\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What was the vote count for the decision?\", \"answer\": \"unanimous\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-23T17:00:44.020Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-23T17:08:40.165Z","sessionId":"c4010bcb-0a05-4118-bee2-613687d082b6","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Download Financial Documents"},{"id":"task-1774285265112-g0ooo0rfg","prompt":"Search the web for real-world SCIENTIFIC and GOVERNMENT documents (research papers, NIST standards, government reports) and download them to the appropriate subfolder. These will be used in an agentic RAG eval benchmark.\n\n## Your Goal\nFind 3-4 publicly available scientific or government documents with clear facts, statistics, and structured content. Save to:\n- `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\scientific\\` — for research papers / academic docs\n- `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\government\\` — for government standards / reports\n\n## Good Candidates (use WebSearch + WebFetch)\n\n### Scientific\n1. **An arXiv paper abstract+introduction** — search for \"attention is all you need arxiv abstract\" and fetch https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762 (the HTML abstract page, not the PDF). This is the famous Transformer paper.\n2. **A CDC or WHO disease statistics page** — search \"CDC influenza statistics 2023-2024 season\" and fetch a summary page\n\n### Government\n1. **NIST Cybersecurity Framework summary** — search \"NIST CSF 2.0 executive summary site:nist.gov\" and fetch the page. Or try https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework\n2. **US Bureau of Labor Statistics report** — search \"BLS employment situation summary 2024 site:bls.gov\" and fetch a recent press release. E.g., https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm\n3. **EPA air quality or climate fact sheet** — search \"EPA climate change fact sheet 2024\" and fetch a short factual page\n\n## Steps\n1. Use WebSearch to find the right URLs\n2. Use WebFetch to retrieve content\n3. Save to the appropriate subfolder (scientific or government)\n4. Keep files under 50KB — extract most fact-dense sections if needed\n5. Use descriptive filenames: `attention_transformer_arxiv.txt`, `nist_csf_overview.txt`, `bls_employment_2024.txt`\n\n## IMPORTANT for saving files\n- Use complete absolute Windows paths with backslashes\n- Read each file back after writing to verify\n\n## Output Required\nReturn a JSON block listing each file and 2-3 ground-truth facts:\n```json\n{\n  \"documents\": [\n    {\n      \"filename\": \"attention_transformer_arxiv.txt\",\n      \"folder\": \"scientific\",\n      \"format\": \"text\",\n      \"domain\": \"scientific\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762\",\n      \"facts\": [\n        {\"question\": \"What year was the 'Attention Is All You Need' paper published?\", \"answer\": \"2017\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What architecture does the paper propose?\", \"answer\": \"The Transformer\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What task does the model achieve state-of-the-art on?\", \"answer\": \"Machine translation (English-to-German and English-to-French)\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-23T17:01:05.353Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-23T17:08:15.876Z","sessionId":"bdaa171f-89b2-4519-afd7-ac247c89097b","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Download Scientific/Gov Documents"},{"id":"task-1774285401520-vyux4n16z","prompt":"Create realistic real-world XLSX spreadsheet files for an agentic RAG eval benchmark. Save them to `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\spreadsheets\\`.\n\n## Goal\nCreate 3-4 XLSX files populated with REAL data (sourced from public web data) covering these scenarios:\n1. **Financial model** — quarterly income statement with real-ish numbers\n2. **Government statistics** — real employment or economic data from BLS/Census\n3. **Product inventory / sales** — multi-sheet workbook with lookup tables\n4. **Budget tracker** — department budget vs actuals\n\n## Step 1: Search for Real Data\nUse WebSearch + WebFetch to get actual numbers from:\n- BLS Employment Situation: https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm (unemployment rates, payroll numbers)\n- US Census quick facts: https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/US/PST045223\n- Any public CSV dataset from data.gov or similar\n\nIncorporate those real numbers into the spreadsheets so they have genuine ground truth.\n\n## Step 2: Create the XLSX Files Using Python + openpyxl\n\nRun a Python script using `uv run python` (NOT `python` — this is Windows and `python` is not in PATH).\n\nCreate a script at `C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\spreadsheets\\gen_xlsx.py` and run it.\n\n```python\nimport openpyxl\nfrom openpyxl.styles import Font, PatternFill, Alignment\nfrom openpyxl.utils import get_column_letter\n# ... create each workbook ...\n```\n\n### File 1: `company_financials_2024.xlsx`\n- Sheet \"Income Statement\": quarterly revenue, COGS, gross profit, operating expenses, net income for Q1-Q4 2024\n- Sheet \"Balance Sheet\": assets, liabilities, equity at year end  \n- Use REAL company-scale numbers (tens of millions range)\n- Include totals row and YoY comparison column\n- Ground truth facts: Q4 revenue, full-year net income, gross margin %\n\n### File 2: `us_labor_statistics_2024.xlsx`  \n- Sheet \"Monthly Unemployment\": month, unemployment rate (%), labor force participation rate, nonfarm payrolls — use REAL 2024 BLS data fetched from the web\n- Sheet \"Industry Breakdown\": major industries, employment count, monthly change\n- Ground truth: December 2024 unemployment rate, total nonfarm payrolls, best month for job gains\n\n### File 3: `product_inventory.xlsx`\n- Sheet \"Inventory\": SKU, product name, category, unit cost, quantity on hand, reorder point, supplier\n- Sheet \"Price List\": SKU, MSRP, discount tier, margin %\n- Sheet \"Lookup\": use VLOOKUP-style reference data\n- 30-50 product rows, realistic electronics/hardware products\n- Ground truth: total inventory value, out-of-stock items, highest-margin product\n\n### File 4: `department_budget_2024.xlsx`\n- Sheet \"Budget vs Actual\": department, Q1-Q4 budgeted, Q1-Q4 actual, variance, % over/under\n- Sheet \"Headcount\": department, headcount, avg salary, total labor cost\n- Include a Summary sheet with department totals and YTD figures\n- Ground truth: which department was most over budget, total headcount, largest variance\n\n## IMPORTANT\n- Run with: `uv run python C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4\\eval\\corpus\\real_world\\spreadsheets\\gen_xlsx.py`\n- Check the script runs without errors\n- Read back file listing to confirm all 4 XLSX files were created\n- Clean up the gen_xlsx.py script after files are confirmed\n\n## Output Required\nReturn a JSON block with each file and its ground-truth facts:\n```json\n{\n  \"documents\": [\n    {\n      \"filename\": \"company_financials_2024.xlsx\",\n      \"format\": \"xlsx\",\n      \"domain\": \"financial\",\n      \"sheets\": [\"Income Statement\", \"Balance Sheet\"],\n      \"facts\": [\n        {\"question\": \"What was Q4 2024 revenue?\", \"answer\": \"$X million\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What was the full-year net income?\", \"answer\": \"$X million\"},\n        {\"question\": \"What was the gross margin in Q2?\", \"answer\": \"XX%\"}\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia4","createdAt":"2026-03-23T17:03:21.794Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-23T17:16:02.637Z","sessionId":null,"systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- Use `claudia_rename_task` to update your own task name if your work evolves beyond the original prompt\n\n**Auto-title your task:**\n- Early in your work (after understanding the task), use `claudia_rename_task` to give YOUR OWN task a short, descriptive title (3-6 words) that reflects what you're actually doing\n- Your task ID is provided in the `claudia_rename_task` tool description — use it to rename yourself\n- If the user has manually edited your task title, the rename will be rejected — do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, update your title to reflect the new focus (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Create XLSX eval benchmark files"},{"id":"task-1773895139670-rjirlwe90","prompt":"can you merge with head of main?","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia7","createdAt":"2026-03-19T04:39:00.395Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-30T22:08:27.515Z","sessionId":"c37aa492-4782-486e-8ef4-86ccbe5b060e","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a tes