ename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775080396486-nbg7d5iwe\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775080396486-nbg7d5iwe):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775080396486-nbg7d5iwe\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775080396486-nbg7d5iwe\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":4168145,"displayName":"Activity panel + sidebar unread"},{"id":"task-1775065908599-s3d7k92ll","prompt":"what are some ideas on how to enable claudia to collaborate with multiple developers in a team?","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\claudia","createdAt":"2026-04-01T17:51:48.700Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-01T22:39:19.293Z","sessionId":"6a0ce63d-fb77-404c-9de9-a69a3319fa1a","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775065908599-s3d7k92ll\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775065908599-s3d7k92ll):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775065908599-s3d7k92ll\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775065908599-s3d7k92ll\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":2556017,"displayName":"Team collaboration ideas"},{"id":"task-1774426195713-7e8172ocu","prompt":"the claude code terminal is still broken","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\claudia","createdAt":"2026-03-25T08:09:56.336Z","lastActivity":"2026-03-30T21:51:10.787Z","sessionId":"5bc8a573-d374-4bdf-89fa-26a88ae2a68e","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774426195713-7e8172ocu\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1774426195713-7e8172ocu):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1774426195713-7e8172ocu\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1774426195713-7e8172ocu\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Debug terminal garbled text"},{"id":"task-1775088844508-wq43iyigu","prompt":"Minor change, while the agent is thinking/streaming output, allow the user to still enter text in the chat box.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-02T00:14:04.609Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-02T00:17:31.602Z","sessionId":"ffb51658-6fea-4c94-92b2-cf18dc17d38f","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775088844508-wq43iyigu\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775088844508-wq43iyigu):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775088844508-wq43iyigu\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775088844508-wq43iyigu\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0},{"id":"task-1775082278105-oks2okzcs","prompt":"## Task: Implement Memory v2 Unit Tests\n\nYou are implementing comprehensive unit tests for GAIA's Memory v2 system. The complete architecture spec is at:\n`docs/spec/agent-memory-architecture.md` — READ THIS ENTIRE FILE FIRST to understand all v2 features.\n\nStudy the existing test patterns:\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_store.py` — existing MemoryStore tests (extend these)\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_mixin.py` — existing MemoryMixin tests (extend these)\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_router.py` — existing router tests (extend these)\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_discovery.py` — existing discovery tests (leave as-is)\n- `tests/conftest.py` — shared fixtures\n\n### Branch\nWork on branch `feature/agent-memory`.\n\n### Scope\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_store.py` — ADD new test classes for v2 features\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_mixin.py` — ADD new test classes for v2 features\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_router.py` — ADD new test classes for v2 endpoints\n\n### What to test\n\n**1. MemoryStore v2 Tests (test_memory_store.py)**\n\nAdd new test classes (don't modify existing tests):\n\n```python\nclass TestSchemaV2Migration:\n    \"\"\"Test schema migration from v1 to v2.\"\"\"\n    - test_migration_adds_embedding_column\n    - test_migration_adds_superseded_by_column\n    - test_migration_adds_consolidated_at_column\n    - test_migration_creates_new_indexes\n    - test_migration_idempotent (running twice doesn't error)\n    - test_existing_data_preserved_after_migration\n\nclass TestStoreEmbedding:\n    \"\"\"Test embedding storage and retrieval.\"\"\"\n    - test_store_embedding_success\n    - test_store_embedding_nonexistent_id_returns_false\n    - test_store_embedding_overwrites_existing\n    - test_embedding_survives_roundtrip (store bytes, read bytes, compare)\n\nclass TestSupersededBy:\n    \"\"\"Test fact lineage via superseded_by.\"\"\"\n    - test_update_sets_superseded_by\n    - test_search_excludes_superseded_items\n    - test_get_by_category_excludes_superseded\n    - test_get_all_knowledge_excludes_superseded_by_default\n    - test_get_all_knowledge_includes_superseded_when_requested\n    - test_superseded_chain (A superseded by B superseded by C)\n    - test_system_prompt_excludes_superseded\n\nclass TestTemporalSearch:\n    \"\"\"Test time_from/time_to filtering.\"\"\"\n    - test_search_with_time_from_only\n    - test_search_with_time_to_only\n    - test_search_with_time_range\n    - test_search_time_range_empty_result\n    - test_search_time_range_with_category_filter\n    - test_search_time_range_with_context_filter\n\nclass TestConsolidationQueries:\n    \"\"\"Test consolidation-related queries.\"\"\"\n    - test_get_unconsolidated_sessions_returns_eligible\n    - test_get_unconsolidated_sessions_respects_age_threshold\n    - test_get_unconsolidated_sessions_respects_min_turns\n    - test_get_unconsolidated_sessions_excludes_already_consolidated\n    - test_mark_turns_consolidated_sets_timestamp\n    - test_mark_turns_consolidated_returns_count\n    - test_consolidated_turns_not_returned_by_get_unconsolidated\n\nclass TestReconciliationQueries:\n    \"\"\"Test reconciliation support queries.\"\"\"\n    - test_get_items_for_reconciliation_returns_active_with_embeddings\n    - test_get_items_for_reconciliation_excludes_superseded\n    - test_get_items_for_reconciliation_excludes_no_embedding\n    - test_get_items_for_reconciliation_respects_context_filter\n\nclass TestSearchByVector:\n    \"\"\"Test vector search data retrieval.\"\"\"\n    - test_search_by_vector_returns_items_with_embeddings\n    - test_search_by_vector_filters_superseded\n    - test_search_by_vector_filters_sensitive\n    - test_search_by_vector_filters_time_range\n    - test_search_by_vector_respects_context\n\nclass TestUseCountIncrement:\n    \"\"\"Test use_count is incremented alongside confidence bump.\"\"\"\n    - test_search_increments_use_count\n    - test_search_increments_use_count_atomically\n```\n\n**2. MemoryMixin v2 Tests (test_memory_mixin.py)**\n\nAdd new test classes:\n\n```python\nclass TestEmbeddingPipeline:\n    \"\"\"Test embedding pipeline.\"\"\"\n    - test_embed_text_returns_numpy_array (mock LemonadeProvider)\n    - test_embed_text_raises_on_lemonade_unavailable\n    - test_backfill_embeddings_embeds_missing_items\n    - test_backfill_respects_limit\n\nclass TestHybridSearch:\n    \"\"\"Test hybrid search orchestration.\"\"\"\n    - test_hybrid_search_combines_vector_and_bm25 (mock FAISS + FTS5)\n    - test_hybrid_search_applies_rrf_fusion\n    - test_hybrid_search_applies_cross_encoder_reranking\n    - test_hybrid_search_deduplicates_by_id\n    - test_hybrid_search_with_time_range\n    - test_hybrid_search_bumps_confidence_and_use_count\n\nclass TestComplexityAwareRecall:\n    \"\"\"Test adaptive top_k.\"\"\"\n    - test_simple_query_returns_top_3\n    - test_medium_query_returns_top_5\n    - test_complex_query_returns_top_10\n    - test_classify_query_simple (< 8 words)\n    - test_classify_query_medium (how/why/explain)\n    - test_classify_query_complex (compare/across/everything)\n\nclass TestMem0Extraction:\n    \"\"\"Test Mem0-style LLM extraction pipeline.\"\"\"\n    - test_extract_add_operation (mock LLM returns add)\n    - test_extract_update_operation_sets_superseded_by\n    - test_extract_delete_operation\n    - test_extract_noop_skipped\n    - test_extract_invalid_json_logged_and_skipped\n    - test_extract_skips_short_turns (< 20 words)\n    - test_extract_fetches_existing_items_first\n\nclass TestConsolidation:\n    \"\"\"Test session consolidation.\"\"\"\n    - test_consolidate_produces_summary_note\n    - test_consolidate_extracts_knowledge_items\n    - test_consolidate_marks_turns\n    - test_consolidate_respects_max_sessions\n    - test_consolidate_skips_recent_sessions\n\nclass TestReconciliation:\n    \"\"\"Test background memory reconciliation.\"\"\"\n    - test_reconcile_detects_contradictions\n    - test_reconcile_reinforces_similar_items\n    - test_reconcile_weakens_partially_outdated\n    - test_reconcile_neutral_no_action\n    - test_reconcile_respects_max_pairs\n\nclass TestStartupSequence:\n    \"\"\"Test init_memory() v2 startup order.\"\"\"\n    - test_startup_validates_lemonade (mock unavailable -> RuntimeError)\n    - test_startup_backfills_embeddings\n    - test_startup_rebuilds_faiss\n    - test_startup_runs_decay_before_reconcile\n    - test_startup_runs_reconcile_before_consolidate\n    - test_startup_runs_consolidate_before_prune\n\nclass TestRecallToolV2:\n    \"\"\"Test updated recall tool.\"\"\"\n    - test_recall_with_time_from_to\n    - test_recall_uses_hybrid_search\n    - test_recall_adaptive_top_k\n\nclass TestRememberToolV2:\n    \"\"\"Test remember tool embeds after store.\"\"\"\n    - test_remember_embeds_new_item\n```\n\n**3. Router v2 Tests (test_memory_router.py)**\n\nAdd new test classes:\n\n```python\nclass TestConsolidateEndpoint:\n    - test_post_consolidate_returns_stats\n    - test_post_consolidate_runs_consolidation\n\nclass TestRebuildEmbeddingsEndpoint:\n    - test_post_rebuild_returns_stats\n\nclass TestReconcileEndpoint:\n    - test_post_reconcile_returns_stats\n\nclass TestEmbeddingCoverageEndpoint:\n    - test_get_coverage_returns_stats\n\nclass TestKnowledgeSupersededFilter:\n    - test_list_knowledge_excludes_superseded_by_default\n    - test_list_knowledge_includes_superseded_when_requested\n\nclass TestKnowledgeTemporalFilter:\n    - test_list_knowledge_with_time_from\n    - test_list_knowledge_with_time_range\n```\n\n### Testing Patterns\n- Use `tmp_path` for isolated SQLite databases\n- Mock LemonadeProvider for embedding calls\n- Mock LLM responses for extraction/consolidation/reconciliation\n- Use `pytest.raises(RuntimeError)` for hard requirement tests\n- Use completely distinct vocabulary per test entry to avoid dedup false positives (80% overlap threshold)\n- Follow existing test patterns in the files\n\n### Validation\nRun all tests: `uv run python -m pytest tests/unit/test_memory_store.py tests/unit/test_memory_mixin.py tests/unit/test_memory_router.py -x --tb=short -q`\nAll tests must pass.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-01T22:24:38.221Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-02T00:15:05.581Z","sessionId":"3886f63c-624e-4415-98de-3c25176740c1","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082278105-oks2okzcs\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775082278105-oks2okzcs):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082278105-oks2okzcs\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775082278105-oks2okzcs\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Memory v2 Unit Tests"},{"id":"task-1775082326429-sd7f2vhwn","prompt":"## Task: Implement Memory v2 Data Layer (memory_store.py)\n\nYou are implementing the data layer for GAIA's Memory v2 system. The complete architecture spec is at:\n`docs/spec/agent-memory-architecture.md` — READ THIS ENTIRE FILE FIRST before writing any code.\n\n### Branch\nWork on branch `feature/agent-memory`. The current code is at `src/gaia/agents/base/memory_store.py`.\n\n### Scope: memory_store.py ONLY\nYou are modifying ONLY `src/gaia/agents/base/memory_store.py`. Do not modify other files.\n\n### What to implement\n\n**1. Schema Migration v1 → v2**\n- Add `embedding BLOB` column to knowledge table (float32 vector, NULL = not yet embedded)\n- Add `superseded_by TEXT` column to knowledge table (ID of newer item that replaced this, NULL = active)\n- Add `consolidated_at TEXT` column to conversations table (ISO 8601 when distilled to knowledge)\n- Add new indexes: `idx_knowledge_no_embedding`, `idx_knowledge_superseded`, `idx_conv_not_consolidated`\n- Schema version tracking: update schema_version table from 1 to 2\n- Migration runs automatically in `__init__` if schema_version < 2\n- Use ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN for each new column (SQLite supports this)\n\n**2. New Methods**\n\n```python\n# Embedding storage\ndef store_embedding(self, knowledge_id: str, embedding: bytes) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Store embedding BLOB for a knowledge item. Returns True if updated.\"\"\"\n\n# Vector search data retrieval (MemoryMixin does the actual FAISS comparison)\ndef get_items_with_embeddings(self, category=None, context=None, entity=None,\n                               include_sensitive=False, top_k=100,\n                               time_from=None, time_to=None) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Return active knowledge items with their stored embeddings.\n    Filters: superseded_by IS NULL, context, category, entity, sensitive, time range.\n    Returns items with all fields INCLUDING embedding BLOB.\n    Used by MemoryMixin to build/query FAISS index.\"\"\"\n\ndef get_items_without_embeddings(self, limit=100) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Return items where embedding IS NULL. For backfill on startup.\"\"\"\n\n# Consolidation\ndef get_unconsolidated_sessions(self, older_than_days=14, min_turns=5, limit=5) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Find session_ids eligible for consolidation.\n    Criteria: all turns older than older_than_days, at least min_turns, \n    at least one turn with consolidated_at IS NULL.\"\"\"\n\ndef mark_turns_consolidated(self, turn_ids: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Set consolidated_at=now on specified turns. Returns count marked.\"\"\"\n\n# Reconciliation support\ndef get_items_for_reconciliation(self, context=None, limit=100) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Get active knowledge items with embeddings for pairwise comparison.\n    Filters: superseded_by IS NULL, embedding IS NOT NULL.\n    Returns items with all fields including embedding.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**3. Update Existing Methods**\n\n- `search()`: Add `time_from: str = None, time_to: str = None` parameters. Add `WHERE created_at >= ?` and `WHERE created_at <= ?` when provided. Add `WHERE superseded_by IS NULL` to the base query. Increment `use_count` alongside confidence bump (atomic SQL: `use_count = use_count + 1`).\n\n- `update()`: Add `superseded_by: str = None` parameter. When provided, set `superseded_by = ?`.\n\n- `get_by_category()`: Add `WHERE superseded_by IS NULL` filter.\n\n- `get_by_category_contexts()`: Add `WHERE superseded_by IS NULL` filter.\n\n- `get_by_entity()`: Add `WHERE superseded_by IS NULL` filter.\n\n- `get_upcoming()`: Add `WHERE superseded_by IS NULL` filter.\n\n- `get_all_knowledge()`: Add `include_superseded: bool = False` parameter. Default: filter `superseded_by IS NULL`. When True: include all items, add `superseded_by` to returned dict.\n\n- `get_sessions()`: Ensure this method exists (may need to add if missing from class interface). Returns session list with turn counts, timestamps, first message preview.\n\n- `get_entities()`: Ensure this method exists with `limit: int = 100` parameter.\n\n- `get_contexts()`: Ensure this method exists with `limit: int = 100` parameter.\n\n**4. Key Design Rules**\n- MemoryStore is a PURE DATA LAYER — no imports from gaia.agents, no LLM calls, no FAISS\n- All writes wrapped in try/except with self._conn.rollback() (existing pattern)\n- Thread-safe via existing threading.Lock\n- WAL mode for concurrent reads\n- All timestamps: `datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat()`\n- Embedding stored as BLOB (numpy float32 array via `embedding.tobytes()`)\n- NEVER use bare `json.loads()` — use `_safe_json_loads()` helper\n\n**5. Schema Migration Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef _migrate_schema(self):\n    \"\"\"Apply schema migrations based on schema_version.\"\"\"\n    current_version = self._get_schema_version()\n    \n    if current_version < 2:\n        # v1 -> v2: Add embedding, superseded_by, consolidated_at\n        with self._lock:\n            try:\n                self._conn.execute(\"ALTER TABLE knowledge ADD COLUMN embedding BLOB\")\n                self._conn.execute(\"ALTER TABLE knowledge ADD COLUMN superseded_by TEXT\")\n                self._conn.execute(\"ALTER TABLE conversations ADD COLUMN consolidated_at TEXT\")\n                self._conn.execute(\n                    \"CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_knowledge_no_embedding \"\n                    \"ON knowledge(id) WHERE embedding IS NULL\"\n                )\n                self._conn.execute(\n                    \"CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_knowledge_superseded \"\n                    \"ON knowledge(superseded_by) WHERE superseded_by IS NOT NULL\"\n                )\n                self._conn.execute(\n                    \"CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_conv_not_consolidated \"\n                    \"ON conversations(session_id) WHERE consolidated_at IS NULL\"\n                )\n                self._conn.execute(\n                    \"UPDATE schema_version SET version = 2, migrated_at = ?\",\n                    (_now_iso(),)\n                )\n                self._conn.commit()\n            except Exception:\n                self._conn.rollback()\n                raise\n```\n\n### Testing\nAfter implementation, run: `uv run python -m pytest tests/unit/test_memory_store.py -x --tb=short -q`\nFix any failures before completing.\n\n### Review\nRead the spec's \"Known Corner Cases and Fixes\" section — ensure your changes don't regress any of the 13 documented fixes. Especially:\n- All write paths must have try/except with rollback\n- FTS5 sync must be maintained for any changed queries\n- Confidence bumps must use SQL-side arithmetic (not Python-side)\n- `superseded_by IS NULL` filter must not break existing FTS5 JOIN queries","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-01T22:25:26.539Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-01T22:55:59.301Z","sessionId":"3e939fa2-4552-4ad3-8963-35fb0b561f7d","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082326429-sd7f2vhwn\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775082326429-sd7f2vhwn):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082326429-sd7f2vhwn\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775082326429-sd7f2vhwn\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Implement Memory v2 Data Layer"},{"id":"task-1775082184928-x0fgmfkbg","prompt":"## Task: Implement Memory v2 Mixin (memory.py)\n\nYou are implementing the agent integration layer for GAIA's Memory v2 system. The complete architecture spec is at:\n`docs/spec/agent-memory-architecture.md` — READ THIS ENTIRE FILE FIRST before writing any code.\n\n### Branch\nWork on branch `feature/agent-memory`. The current code is at `src/gaia/agents/base/memory.py`.\n\n### Scope: memory.py ONLY\nYou are modifying ONLY `src/gaia/agents/base/memory.py`. Do not modify memory_store.py or other files.\n\n### What to implement\n\n**1. Embedding Pipeline**\n\n```python\ndef _get_embedder(self) -> Any:\n    \"\"\"Lazy-init cached LemonadeProvider for embeddings. NOT optional — raises RuntimeError if unavailable.\"\"\"\n\ndef _embed_text(self, text: str) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"Embed text via Lemonade (nomic-embed-text-v2-moe-GGUF, 768-dim). Required, not optional.\"\"\"\n\ndef _backfill_embeddings(self, limit: int = 100) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Embed items missing embeddings. Called on startup. Returns count backfilled.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**2. FAISS Index Lifecycle**\n- Import faiss (faiss-cpu). Use IndexFlatIP (inner product on L2-normalized vectors = cosine similarity)\n- `_rebuild_faiss_index()` — build from stored embedding BLOBs on init\n- Incremental updates: add on store(), remove on delete(), replace on update()\n- Store a mapping: faiss_index_position → knowledge_id\n\n**3. Hybrid Search Orchestration**\nThe MemoryMixin orchestrates hybrid search by:\n1. Embedding the query via `_embed_text()`\n2. FAISS cosine search: top_k × 4 candidates\n3. FTS5 BM25 via `self._memory_store.search()`: top_k × 4 candidates\n4. Deduplicate by ID, apply RRF fusion: `score = 0.6 / (60 + rank_vector) + 0.4 / (60 + rank_bm25)`\n5. Cross-encoder reranking via `cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2` (~22MB, sentence-transformers)\n6. Return final top_k\n7. Bump confidence + use_count\n\n```python\ndef _hybrid_search(self, query: str, category=None, context=None, entity=None,\n                   include_sensitive=False, top_k=5, time_from=None, time_to=None) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Full hybrid search: vector + BM25 + RRF + cross-encoder reranking.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**4. Complexity-Aware Recall Depth**\n```python\ndef _classify_query_complexity(self, query: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Returns adaptive top_k: 3 (simple), 5 (medium), 10 (complex).\n    Simple: < 8 words, single entity. Medium: 8-20 words or how/why/explain.\n    Complex: > 20 words or compare/across/all/history/everything.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**5. Mem0-Style LLM Extraction**\nReplace `_extract_heuristics()` with `_extract_via_llm()`. The extraction prompt is in the spec. Key design:\n- Fetch top-10 existing items via `_hybrid_search(conversation_text)`\n- Single LLM call with conversation + existing memory → operations (ADD/UPDATE/DELETE/NOOP)\n- Execute operations: ADD → store(), UPDATE → store new + update old with superseded_by, DELETE → delete()\n- Source: \"llm_extract\", confidence: 0.4\n\n```python\ndef _extract_via_llm(self, user_input: str, assistant_response: str,\n                     existing_items: List[Dict]) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Mem0-style extraction. Returns list of operation dicts with op field.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**6. Conversation Consolidation**\n```python\ndef consolidate_old_sessions(self, max_sessions: int = 5) -> Dict:\n    \"\"\"Distill old sessions (>14 days, >=5 turns) into knowledge items.\n    Returns {consolidated: int, extracted_items: int}.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**7. Background Memory Reconciliation**\n```python\ndef reconcile_memory(self, max_pairs: int = 20) -> Dict:\n    \"\"\"Pairwise similarity check (>0.85 cosine) on active items.\n    LLM classifies: reinforce/contradict/weaken/neutral.\n    Returns {pairs_checked, reinforced, contradicted, weakened, neutral}.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**8. Updated Startup Sequence**\nUpdate `init_memory()` to follow this exact order:\n1. Open/create DB, apply schema migrations\n2. Validate Lemonade embedding service connectivity — `RuntimeError` if unreachable\n3. Backfill embeddings for items missing them\n4. Rebuild FAISS index from stored embeddings\n5. apply_confidence_decay()\n6. reconcile_memory() (max 20 pairs)\n7. consolidate_old_sessions() (max 5 sessions)\n8. prune()\n9. Generate session UUID\n\n**9. Updated Memory Tools**\n- `recall` tool: add `time_from`, `time_to` parameters. Use `_classify_query_complexity()` for adaptive top_k. Route through `_hybrid_search()`.\n- `search_past_conversations` tool: add `time_from`, `time_to` parameters.\n- `remember` tool: after store(), embed the new item via `_embed_text()` and `store_embedding()`.\n\n**10. Updated _after_process_query()**\nReplace heuristic extraction with Mem0-style LLM extraction:\n1. Store conversation turns (unchanged)\n2. For turns >= 20 words: fetch existing items, call `_extract_via_llm()`, execute operations\n3. No regex fallback — if extraction fails, log error and skip\n\n### Key Design Rules\n- No silent fallback. Embeddings are HARD REQUIREMENT.\n- Cross-encoder loaded lazily via sentence-transformers, cached for process lifetime.\n- FAISS index is in-memory, rebuilt from SQLite BLOBs on startup.\n- All new items must be embedded immediately after store().\n\n### Dependencies\n- `faiss-cpu` (already in setup.py rag extras)\n- `numpy` (already a dependency)\n- `sentence-transformers` (already in setup.py rag extras)\n- LemonadeProvider from `gaia.llm.providers.lemonade`\n\n### Testing\nAfter implementation, run: `uv run python -m pytest tests/unit/test_memory_mixin.py -x --tb=short -q`\nFix any failures before completing.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-01T22:23:05.037Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-02T02:25:43.414Z","sessionId":"49372ec0-4865-46e1-bec5-7d0c473854bf","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082184928-x0fgmfkbg\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775082184928-x0fgmfkbg):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082184928-x0fgmfkbg\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775082184928-x0fgmfkbg\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":2672434,"displayName":"Final Review, Test & Commit memory.py"},{"id":"task-1775082549125-eb9mw2f2b","prompt":"## Task: Implement Memory v2 Integration Tests\n\nYou are implementing integration tests for GAIA's Memory v2 system that test the full pipeline with real components (real SQLite, real FAISS, real FTS5) and only mock the external LLM service. The complete architecture spec is at:\n`docs/spec/agent-memory-architecture.md` — READ THIS ENTIRE FILE FIRST.\n\nStudy existing test patterns:\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_store.py` — unit test patterns\n- `tests/unit/test_memory_mixin.py` — mixin test patterns with MockAgent\n- `tests/conftest.py` — shared fixtures\n\n### Branch\nWork on branch `feature/agent-memory`.\n\n### Scope\n- `tests/integration/test_memory_integration.py` — NEW file for end-to-end memory pipeline tests\n- `tests/integration/test_memory_api_integration.py` — NEW file for REST API integration tests\n\nCreate the `tests/integration/` directory if it doesn't exist.\n\n### What to test\n\n**1. Full Pipeline Integration (test_memory_integration.py)**\n\nThese tests use REAL SQLite + REAL FAISS + REAL numpy but MOCK the LLM (Lemonade) for embeddings and extraction. Use deterministic fake embeddings (random vectors with fixed seed) to test the full search pipeline.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport pytest\n\n# Fixture: create a real MemoryStore with v2 schema\n@pytest.fixture\ndef memory_store(tmp_path):\n    \"\"\"Real SQLite MemoryStore with v2 schema.\"\"\"\n    from gaia.agents.base.memory_store import MemoryStore\n    store = MemoryStore(db_path=tmp_path / \"test_memory.db\")\n    return store\n\n# Fixture: deterministic embeddings (768-dim, normalized)\n@pytest.fixture\ndef fake_embedder():\n    \"\"\"Returns a function that generates deterministic embeddings from text.\"\"\"\n    def embed(text: str) -> np.ndarray:\n        rng = np.random.RandomState(hash(text) % 2**32)\n        vec = rng.randn(768).astype(np.float32)\n        vec /= np.linalg.norm(vec)  # L2 normalize for cosine similarity\n        return vec\n    return embed\n\n\nclass TestFullStorageAndRetrieval:\n    \"\"\"End-to-end: store items with embeddings, search via hybrid.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_store_embed_search_roundtrip(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store 10 items with embeddings, verify hybrid search returns relevant results.\"\"\"\n        # Store items across different categories and contexts\n        # Embed each item\n        # Search with a query that should match specific items\n        # Verify correct items returned in correct order\n    \n    def test_superseded_items_excluded_from_search(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store item A, supersede with item B, verify only B found in search.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_temporal_filtering_with_hybrid_search(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store items at different timestamps, verify time_from/time_to filtering works\n        correctly with both vector and BM25 components.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_context_isolation_in_hybrid_search(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store items in work/personal contexts, verify search respects context boundaries.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_sensitive_items_excluded_from_default_search(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store sensitive + non-sensitive items, verify only non-sensitive returned by default.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_confidence_and_use_count_updated_on_search(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Verify both confidence (+0.02) and use_count (+1) increment atomically on recall.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_dedup_with_embeddings(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store similar content (>80% word overlap), verify dedup works and embedding is updated.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestFAISSIndexLifecycle:\n    \"\"\"Test FAISS index build, update, delete operations.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_build_index_from_stored_embeddings(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store 50 items with embeddings, build FAISS index, verify search works.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_incremental_add_to_index(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Build index, add new item, verify it's immediately searchable.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_incremental_delete_from_index(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Build index, delete item, verify it's no longer returned in search.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_index_survives_rebuild(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Build index, close store, reopen, rebuild from BLOBs, verify same results.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_index_with_100_items_performance(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store 100 items, verify search completes in < 100ms.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestSchemaV2Migration:\n    \"\"\"Test actual schema migration on a v1 database.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_migrate_v1_database_preserves_data(self, tmp_path):\n        \"\"\"Create a v1 database with data, migrate to v2, verify all data intact\n        and new columns exist with NULL defaults.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_fresh_database_creates_v2_schema(self, tmp_path):\n        \"\"\"New database should have all v2 columns from the start.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_migration_adds_all_indexes(self, tmp_path):\n        \"\"\"Verify all v2 indexes exist after migration.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestConsolidationPipeline:\n    \"\"\"End-to-end consolidation with real DB operations.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_full_consolidation_flow(self, memory_store):\n        \"\"\"Create sessions > 14 days old with > 5 turns.\n        Call get_unconsolidated_sessions() to find them.\n        Simulate LLM consolidation output (mock LLM call).\n        Store summary and extracted knowledge.\n        Mark turns consolidated.\n        Verify turns not returned by get_unconsolidated_sessions() again.\n        Verify summary note is searchable.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_consolidation_skips_recent_sessions(self, memory_store):\n        \"\"\"Create sessions < 14 days old, verify not returned for consolidation.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_consolidation_skips_short_sessions(self, memory_store):\n        \"\"\"Create sessions with < 5 turns, verify not returned for consolidation.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_already_consolidated_sessions_skipped(self, memory_store):\n        \"\"\"Mark all turns consolidated, verify session not returned again.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestReconciliationPipeline:\n    \"\"\"End-to-end reconciliation with real DB and FAISS.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_contradictory_items_detected(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store two items with similar embeddings but contradictory content.\n        Run reconciliation (mock LLM classification as 'contradict').\n        Verify older item is superseded.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_reinforcing_items_boosted(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store two items with similar embeddings and reinforcing content.\n        Run reconciliation (mock LLM as 'reinforce').\n        Verify both items get confidence boost.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_reconciliation_rate_limiting(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store 50 items with pairwise similarity > 0.85.\n        Run reconciliation with max_pairs=5.\n        Verify only 5 pairs processed.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestMem0ExtractionPipeline:\n    \"\"\"End-to-end extraction with real DB operations.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_add_operation_stores_new_item(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Mock LLM returns ADD operation. Verify item stored and embedded.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_update_operation_supersedes_old(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store existing item. Mock LLM returns UPDATE.\n        Verify old item has superseded_by set, new item stored.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_delete_operation_removes_item(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store existing item. Mock LLM returns DELETE. Verify item deleted.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_mixed_operations_in_single_extraction(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Mock LLM returns mix of ADD + UPDATE + DELETE.\n        Verify all operations executed correctly.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestRRFFusion:\n    \"\"\"Test RRF score fusion correctness.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_rrf_combines_rankings_correctly(self):\n        \"\"\"Given two rankings, verify RRF formula produces correct merged ranking.\n        score = 0.6 / (60 + rank_vector) + 0.4 / (60 + rank_bm25)\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_item_in_both_rankings_scores_higher(self):\n        \"\"\"Item appearing in both vector and BM25 results should rank above\n        items appearing in only one.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_vector_weight_dominates(self):\n        \"\"\"With 0.6/0.4 weighting, vector rank should have more influence\n        than BM25 rank for the same position.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestComplexityAwareRecall:\n    \"\"\"Test query complexity classification with real queries.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_simple_queries(self):\n        \"\"\"'my timezone', 'user name', 'what time' -> top_k=3\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_medium_queries(self):\n        \"\"\"'how do I deploy the app', 'explain the auth flow' -> top_k=5\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_complex_queries(self):\n        \"\"\"'compare everything Sarah and John said about API migration \n        across all standups last month' -> top_k=10\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestTemporalSearchIntegration:\n    \"\"\"Test temporal filtering with real data at different timestamps.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_recall_last_week(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store items across 3 weeks. Search with time range for 'last week'.\n        Verify only items from that week returned.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_recall_today_only(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Store items today and yesterday. Filter time_from=today.\n        Verify only today's items returned.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_temporal_filter_combines_with_category(self, memory_store, fake_embedder):\n        \"\"\"Filter by both category='note' AND time range. Verify intersection.\"\"\"\n```\n\n**2. REST API Integration Tests (test_memory_api_integration.py)**\n\nThese test the FastAPI endpoints with a real MemoryStore backend.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef api_client(tmp_path):\n    \"\"\"FastAPI test client with real MemoryStore.\"\"\"\n    # Setup: create app with test database\n    # Return TestClient\n\n\nclass TestConsolidateEndpoint:\n    def test_post_consolidate_returns_stats(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"POST /api/memory/consolidate returns {consolidated, extracted_items}.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_consolidate_with_no_eligible_sessions(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"Returns {consolidated: 0} when no sessions are eligible.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestEmbeddingCoverageEndpoint:\n    def test_coverage_with_all_embedded(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"100% coverage when all items have embeddings.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_coverage_with_partial_embedding(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"Correct percentage when some items lack embeddings.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestReconcileEndpoint:\n    def test_post_reconcile_returns_stats(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"POST /api/memory/reconcile returns reconciliation stats.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestKnowledgeFilterEndpoints:\n    def test_exclude_superseded_by_default(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"GET /api/memory/knowledge excludes superseded items by default.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_include_superseded_when_requested(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"GET /api/memory/knowledge?include_superseded=true returns all items.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_temporal_filter_on_knowledge(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"GET /api/memory/knowledge?time_from=X&time_to=Y filters correctly.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass TestStatsEndpointV2:\n    def test_stats_include_embedding_coverage(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"GET /api/memory/stats includes embedding section.\"\"\"\n    \n    def test_stats_include_reconciliation_info(self, api_client):\n        \"\"\"GET /api/memory/stats includes reconciliation section.\"\"\"\n```\n\n### Key Design Rules for Integration Tests\n- Use `tmp_path` for isolated SQLite databases (no shared state between tests)\n- Use REAL numpy and REAL FAISS (faiss-cpu) — don't mock the vector operations\n- Mock ONLY the LLM calls (Lemonade embedding API and chat completion API)\n- Use deterministic fake embeddings via seeded random (hash of content as seed)\n- Test performance bounds where specified (e.g., \"search completes in < 100ms for 100 items\")\n- Use completely distinct vocabulary per test entry to avoid 80% overlap dedup\n- Use `pytest.mark.integration` marker on all tests so they can be run separately\n\n### Validation\nRun: `uv run python -m pytest tests/integration/test_memory_integration.py tests/integration/test_memory_api_integration.py -x --tb=short -q -m integration`\nAll tests must pass.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-01T22:29:09.236Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-01T22:52:28.509Z","sessionId":"6628153b-5ea7-46f2-8ee0-39417910a49e","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082549125-eb9mw2f2b\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775082549125-eb9mw2f2b):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775082549125-eb9mw2f2b\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775082549125-eb9mw2f2b\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Memory v2 Integration Tests"},{"id":"task-1775083429037-p832f0hei","prompt":"## Task: Update Memory User Guide for v2 — Make It Magical\n\nYou are updating the GAIA Memory user guide to cover v2 features. The goal is to make users EXCITED about using memory as a second brain. Short, sweet, magical — not dry documentation.\n\n### Reference Files\n- Architecture spec (READ FIRST): `docs/spec/agent-memory-architecture.md`\n- Current user guide (EDIT THIS): `docs/guides/memory.mdx`\n- SDK reference (for accuracy): `docs/sdk/sdks/memory.mdx`\n\n### Branch\nWork on branch `feature/agent-memory`.\n\n### Scope\nEdit ONLY `docs/guides/memory.mdx`. Keep the Mintlify MDX format exactly as-is (frontmatter, components like Steps, Card, Info, Warning, Tip, Note, CardGroup).\n\n### What to Update\n\n**1. \"How It Works\" section** — Update the 4 principles:\n- Principle 2: Change \"full-text search\" to \"hybrid semantic+keyword search — find memories by meaning, not just exact words\"\n- Principle 3: Change \"Learn silently\" to \"Learn continuously — LLM extraction from every conversation, not just regex patterns\"\n- Add brief mention: \"The agent sees what it already knows and decides what's new, what's changed, and what's contradicted — like Mem0's intelligent memory management.\"\n\n**2. \"Memory Tools\" section** — Update `recall` to mention:\n- Hybrid semantic search (finds concepts, not just keywords)\n- Temporal filtering: `time_from`/`time_to` examples\n- Add example: \"What did we discuss about deployment last week?\" → agent computes time range and searches\n\n**3. Replace \"Heuristic extraction\" subsection** with \"Intelligent Extraction\":\n- LLM-based extraction after each conversation turn\n- Sees existing memory + new conversation → decides ADD/UPDATE/DELETE\n- No regex — the LLM understands context\n- Example: you say \"we switched from React to Vue\" → agent updates the old React fact, doesn't create a duplicate\n\n**4. Add a NEW \"Second Brain Use Cases\" section** — This is the EXCITING part. Make it feel magical. Use real-world examples that users can try immediately for manual testing:\n\n```mdx\n## Your Second Brain\n\nMemory turns GAIA into a personal knowledge system that grows smarter with every conversation. Here are some things you can try right now:\n\n### Daily Journal\n[Example of logging daily work, then recalling it later]\n\n### Meeting Notes  \n[Example of capturing standup notes with per-person facts and deadlines]\n\n### Research & Reading Notes\n[Example of saving article summaries, then finding them by concept later]\n\n### Personal Reminders\n[Example of setting reminders with due dates, recurring commitments]\n\n### Contact Profiles\n[Example of building up knowledge about colleagues via entity linking]\n\n### Error Learning\n[Example of the agent learning from tool failures automatically]\n```\n\nEach use case should be a short, concrete conversation snippet showing the user input and agent behavior. Make them feel like \"wow, I can actually do this.\"\n\n**5. Update \"Memory Dashboard\" section** — Add:\n- Embedding coverage indicator\n- Maintenance actions (Consolidate, Rebuild Embeddings, Reconcile)\n- Superseded items view (toggle to see fact history)\n- Full-page layout description (not a popup)\n\n**6. Update \"How Memory Improves Over Time\"** — Add:\n- Fact lineage: \"When facts change, the old version is preserved with a superseded_by link. You can see the history of how facts evolved.\"\n- Background reconciliation: \"On startup, the agent checks for contradictory facts across sessions and resolves them automatically.\"\n- Session consolidation: \"Old conversations are distilled into durable knowledge items before being pruned.\"\n\n**7. Update confidence table** — Add:\n- `llm_extract` at 0.4 confidence\n- `consolidation` at 0.5 confidence\n- Remove `heuristic` (no longer used)\n\n**8. Add \"Try It Right Now\" section at the top** — A quick 5-minute hands-on walkthrough:\n```\n1. Start: gaia chat\n2. Say: \"Remember that I'm working on the GAIA memory system and I prefer detailed code examples\"\n3. Say: \"What do you know about me?\"\n4. Say: \"Remember: standup tomorrow at 10am, Sarah is presenting the API migration results\"\n5. Start a new session (gaia chat again)\n6. Say: \"What's coming up?\" — the agent should mention the standup\n7. Say: \"What do you know about Sarah?\" — the agent should recall the standup context\n8. Open the dashboard: gaia chat --ui → click the Brain icon\n```\n\n### Writing Style\n- Conversational, not formal\n- Show don't tell — conversation snippets over paragraphs\n- Each feature in ≤5 lines + example\n- Users should feel \"I want to try this right now\"\n- Think of it as a product demo, not a reference manual\n\n### Validation\n- Ensure all MDX components render (Steps, Card, Info, Warning, Tip, Note, CardGroup)\n- Ensure all links are valid (relative paths to other docs)\n- Keep the frontmatter, copyright footer, and Next Steps cards","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-01T22:43:49.141Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-02T02:46:23.628Z","sessionId":"e5f92e24-9a70-4e86-90f7-8d24a7eb850c","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775083429037-p832f0hei\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775083429037-p832f0hei):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775083429037-p832f0hei\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775083429037-p832f0hei\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Update Memory User Guide v2"},{"id":"task-1775083466361-9dciqd549","prompt":"## Task: Update Memory SDK Reference for v2\n\nYou are updating the GAIA Memory SDK reference documentation to cover v2 features. This is the technical developer reference — accurate method signatures, code examples, and architecture details.\n\n### Reference Files\n- Architecture spec (READ FIRST): `docs/spec/agent-memory-architecture.md`\n- Current SDK reference (EDIT THIS): `docs/sdk/sdks/memory.mdx`\n- User guide (for cross-reference): `docs/guides/memory.mdx`\n\n### Branch\nWork on branch `feature/agent-memory`.\n\n### Scope\nEdit ONLY `docs/sdk/sdks/memory.mdx`. Keep the Mintlify MDX format exactly as-is.\n\n### What to Update\n\n**1. Architecture section** — Update the three-layer table and diagram:\n- MemoryMixin now includes: embedding pipeline, FAISS index, hybrid search orchestration, Mem0-style LLM extraction, consolidation, reconciliation\n- MemoryStore now includes: schema v2 (embedding BLOB, superseded_by, consolidated_at), vector data retrieval, temporal filtering, consolidation queries, reconciliation queries\n- Add FAISS and cross-encoder to the diagram\n\n**2. MemoryMixin section** — Add new methods:\n\n```python\n# Embedding Pipeline\n_get_embedder() -> Any                    # Lazy-init LemonadeProvider (required)\n_embed_text(text: str) -> np.ndarray      # 768-dim vector via nomic-embed-text-v2\n_backfill_embeddings(limit=100) -> int    # Embed items missing embeddings\n\n# Hybrid Search\n_hybrid_search(query, category=None, context=None, entity=None,\n               include_sensitive=False, top_k=5,\n               time_from=None, time_to=None) -> List[Dict]\n\n# Complexity-Aware Recall\n_classify_query_complexity(query: str) -> int  # Returns 3/5/10\n\n# Mem0-Style Extraction\n_extract_via_llm(user_input, assistant_response, existing_items) -> List[Dict]\n    # Returns operations: [{op: \"add|update|delete|noop\", ...}]\n\n# Consolidation\nconsolidate_old_sessions(max_sessions=5) -> Dict\n    # Returns {consolidated: int, extracted_items: int}\n\n# Reconciliation\nreconcile_memory(max_pairs=20) -> Dict\n    # Returns {pairs_checked, reinforced, contradicted, weakened, neutral}\n```\n\n**3. init_memory() section** — Update to show v2 startup sequence:\n1. Open/create DB, apply schema migrations (v1→v2)\n2. Validate Lemonade embedding service — RuntimeError if unreachable\n3. Backfill embeddings\n4. Rebuild FAISS index\n5. apply_confidence_decay()\n6. reconcile_memory()\n7. consolidate_old_sessions()\n8. prune()\n9. Generate session UUID\n\n**4. get_memory_system_prompt()** — Add \"Skills\" section to example output:\n```\nSkills:\n  - Deploy workflow: test → build → push → verify (confidence: 0.88)\n```\n\n**5. Lifecycle Hooks** — Update Hook 3 description:\n- Replace \"heuristic extraction (regex)\" with \"Mem0-style LLM extraction (ADD/UPDATE/DELETE/NOOP)\"\n- Mention it fetches existing items first, then LLM decides operations\n\n**6. MemoryStore section** — Update:\n\nSchema: Add `embedding BLOB`, `superseded_by TEXT`, `consolidated_at TEXT` columns\n\nNew methods:\n```python\nstore_embedding(knowledge_id: str, embedding: bytes) -> bool\nget_items_with_embeddings(...) -> List[Dict]\nget_items_without_embeddings(limit=100) -> List[Dict]\nget_unconsolidated_sessions(older_than_days=14, min_turns=5, limit=5) -> List[str]\nmark_turns_consolidated(turn_ids: List[int]) -> int\nget_items_for_reconciliation(context=None, limit=100) -> List[Dict]\nget_sessions(limit=20) -> List[Dict]\nget_entities(limit=100) -> List[Dict]\nget_contexts(limit=100) -> List[Dict]\n```\n\nUpdated methods:\n- `store()`: mention embedding happens after store\n- `search()`: mention hybrid search (vector+BM25+RRF+cross-encoder), add `time_from`/`time_to` params, add `superseded_by IS NULL` filter\n- `update()`: add `superseded_by: str = None` parameter\n- `get_by_category()`, `get_by_entity()`, `get_upcoming()`: add `superseded_by IS NULL` filter note\n- `get_all_knowledge()`: add `include_superseded: bool = False` parameter\n\n**7. Knowledge Sources table** — Update:\n- Add `llm_extract` at 0.4 (\"Auto-extracted by LLM from conversation, Mem0-style\")\n- Add `consolidation` at 0.5 (\"Distilled from old conversation sessions\")\n- Remove `heuristic` row (no longer used)\n\n**8. API Reference tables** — Update both MemoryMixin and MemoryStore method tables with all new methods\n\n**9. Code Examples** — Add a v2 example showing:\n```python\n# Example: Agent with hybrid search and temporal recall\nagent = RememberBot()\nresult = agent.process_query(\"Remember: standup at 10am tomorrow, Sarah presenting API migration\")\n# Memory stores: note (meeting), reminder (due_at), fact (person:sarah entity)\n# All items immediately embedded for semantic search\n\n# Later, in a new session:\nresult = agent.process_query(\"What do I have coming up?\")\n# Dynamic context: [DUE tomorrow] Standup at 10am\n# Agent proactively mentions it\n\nresult = agent.process_query(\"What do we know about the API migration?\")\n# Hybrid search: vector similarity finds \"API migration\" related items\n# Even if stored as \"Sarah presenting API migration results\" — semantic match\n```\n\n### Writing Style\n- Technical and precise — this is developer documentation\n- Show method signatures with full type hints\n- Include parameter tables where helpful\n- Cross-reference the user guide for conceptual explanations\n\n### Validation\n- All MDX components render correctly\n- All cross-links are valid\n- Method signatures match the architecture spec","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-01T22:44:26.486Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-02T02:43:24.207Z","sessionId":"7789fadd-b82d-45c3-a6c7-735e025938c8","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn't have functionality to do the test then add it. you can also use playwright mcp or curl if either of these would be easier. make sure you have enough logging to debug any issues.. if you create any test files, clean them up when you are done.  NEVER TOUCH PORT 4001.. if you are having issues with public apis, lookup examples online. always review your changes for gaps and issues after making extensive changes.\n\n\n## Claudia Multi-Agent Orchestration\n\nYou are running as an agent inside Claudia, a multi-agent orchestrator. You have access to Claudia MCP tools (claudia_*) that let you collaborate with other agents.\n\n**IMPORTANT — Avoid duplicate work:**\n- Before starting any implementation, ALWAYS call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what other agents are already working on in this workspace\n- If another task is already handling a piece of work, do NOT implement it yourself — wait for that task to finish and read its output instead\n- After spawning tasks, your role shifts to **coordinator**: monitor, unblock, and integrate — do NOT start implementing the same work in parallel\n\n**When to spawn parallel tasks:**\n- When your work can be naturally decomposed into independent pieces (e.g., backend + frontend + tests)\n- When you need to research multiple topics simultaneously\n- When building a feature that has separable components\n\n**How to orchestrate:**\n1. Check existing tasks first — call `claudia_list_tasks` to see what's already running\n2. Plan — break remaining work into independent, parallelizable pieces\n3. Spawn — use `claudia_create_task` for each piece with a clear, self-contained prompt that includes all necessary context\n4. Wait and monitor — poll `claudia_get_task_status` periodically (every 30-60s) until tasks complete. Handle any that need input via `claudia_send_input`\n5. Review — use `claudia_get_task_output` to read results from completed tasks\n6. Integrate — review the combined changes for conflicts or integration issues, then fix any problems yourself\n\n**Task naming:**\n- When creating tasks, always provide a short `displayName` (e.g., \"Build API endpoint\", \"Write unit tests\") so tasks are easy to identify in the sidebar\n- If your work evolves, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775083466361-9dciqd549\" and an updated title\n\n**Auto-title your task (YOUR TASK ID IS: task-1775083466361-9dciqd549):**\n- After you have produced your first meaningful response, call `claudia_rename_task` with taskId=\"task-1775083466361-9dciqd549\" and a short descriptive title (3-6 words) reflecting what you're working on\n- **Do NOT call `claudia_rename_task` before producing output** — write your first response first, then title yourself\n- If the rename is rejected (user manually edited the title), do NOT retry\n- If your work evolves significantly, call `claudia_rename_task` again with taskId=\"task-1775083466361-9dciqd549\" and an updated title (unless user-edited)\n\n**Handling file edit conflicts:**\n- If you get an \"Error editing file\" (e.g., content mismatch, file changed on disk), another Claudia task may be editing the same file concurrently\n- When this happens: call `claudia_list_tasks` to check if another task is currently running that might be modifying the same file\n- If another task IS running and likely touching the same file, wait for it to complete by polling `claudia_get_task_status` every 15-30 seconds before retrying your edit\n- Once the other task finishes, re-read the file to get the latest content, then retry your edit\n- Do NOT blindly retry the edit in a loop — always re-read the file first to pick up changes from the other task\n- If no other task is running, re-read the file and retry — the mismatch may be from your own prior edit\n\n**Guidelines:**\n- Prefer 2-4 parallel tasks — don't over-decompose simple work\n- Only spawn tasks when parallelization provides real value; do simple work yourself\n- Each spawned task prompt should be fully self-contained — include file paths, context, and constraints so it can work independently\n- While waiting for spawned tasks, do NOT start implementing features that overlap with what they're doing\n- **NEVER delete or archive completed tasks** — the user wants to review their outputs. After a task completes, just report its status and read its output. Do NOT call `claudia_delete_task` or `claudia_archive_task` on finished tasks.","historySize":0,"displayName":"Update Memory SDK Reference v2"},{"id":"task-1775104227614-k5crk6mxq","prompt":"Using gaia's MCP server, interact with the agent and test is conversational and memory capabilities. use the agent eval framework for memory testing.","workspaceId":"C:\\Users\\14255\\Work\\gaia3","createdAt":"2026-04-02T04:30:28.067Z","lastActivity":"2026-04-02T07:17:05.286Z","sessionId":"397d4aea-0162-4c4d-88cb-f22d79a482d2","systemPrompt":"if it's a new feature, try to use a test cli unless it would be much easier to just have the user do a manual test (this is usually the case for visual features).. if it doesn